+agree with the first on every case anyone tested and disagree on the one nobody did. The JIR
+encoder (`jir/encode.rs`, which writes every item's JIR body in every mode) and the v1 late-IR
+analysis encoder (`late_ir.rs`, run when `js-opt` is not `off`) decode constant memory into a
+`ConstTree` through the same primitives.
+
+**A tag that is a pointer.** The niche of `Option<&T>`, `Option<&str>`, `Option<&[T]>`,
+`Option<fn()>`, `Option<NonNull<T>>`, `Option<Box<T>>` and `Result<(), &str>` is the pointer the
+untagged variant holds. In a constant allocation the bytes of a pointer are only its offset into the
+allocation its provenance names, so `Some(&7)` — `7` at the start of its own allocation — has the
+bytes of `None`. `raw::variant_index` therefore never reads such a tag by its bytes: when the tag's
+bytes carry provenance it applies rustc's own rule (`InterpCx::read_discriminant`): the variant is
+the untagged one if the niche is the single value `0` and the pointer cannot be null (in bounds of
+its allocation, one past the end included, or misaligned for it), and the constant is refused at
+compile time otherwise. rustc's validator rejects a maybe-null pointer in a niche field, so the
+refusal is not reachable from a constant rustc accepts.
+
+**A pointer at a type that only holds one.** A register-sized constant (`ConstValue::Scalar`) whose
+scalar is a pointer need not have a pointer type: `Some(&x)` at `Option<&T>`, `Some(f)` at
+`Option<fn()>`, a `NonNull<T>`, a one-field struct. Such a constant is written back into a scratch
+allocation together with its provenance (`raw::scratch_alloc`) and read at its type, exactly like a
+register-sized integer at a non-primitive type, in the baseline lane and in both encoders. Only
+a constant whose type is itself a reference, raw pointer or function pointer is lowered as a
+pointer directly.
+
+**The link-time scalar decoder.** A register-sized integer constant at an enum type reaches the
+JIR lowering as bits (`Constant::Scalar`), and `jsc-link`'s `constant.rs::scalar_expr` and
+`dce.rs::scalar_variant` decode it from the type table, and so does the JIR interpreter
+(`interp/eval.rs::scalar`), through the one decoder `rcgjs-object` `jir/niche.rs` (the interpreter
+evaluates a fieldless variant and reports a variant whose payload the bits are as unsupported). The
+table records `niche_start` and the untagged variant but not `niche_variants`, which
+`niche_variant` rebuilds by rustc's rule: every variant but the untagged one takes a niche value unless rustc leaves it out as
+*absent* (uninhabited and every field a 1-ZST), so `Dead([u32; 0], Infallible)` keeps its slot;
+uninhabitedness is rustc's layout fact at any depth (`Wrap<Void>`, `(Void,)`, `[Void; 2]`, an enum
+whose variants are all dead). The subtraction wraps at the width of the niche scalar, which
+`niche_width` finds by walking the producer's niche path (`jir/types.rs::niche_facts`, a walk of
+rustc's layout) through the table without peeling anything: a struct's or union's field, a
+tuple's element, an array's element at any index, a pattern type's base (rustc's field 0), and
+for an enum the variant rustc lays out when its layout is one variant — `single_layout_variant`,
+the first variant that is not absent, which in `enum Parsed { Never((), Infallible), Char(char) }`
+is `Char`, not variant 0. When the walk cannot be followed the constant is declined (the baseline
+lane prints it) rather than decoded at a guessed width. A register-sized constant of an enum whose
+layout is one variant (`const P: Parsed = Parsed::Char('w')`) is that variant's payload. The
+payload is decoded through transparent wrappers and pattern types (`Some(NonZero(77))`).
+
