+holding either: a slice is `{ buf, off, len }` over the static's own storage, so it aliases the
+static as the same slice built at run time does: an array static's buffer is its elements' array;
+a static of the element type is one element, the boxed `[value]` of a primitive static, or for an
+aggregate static its one slot's buffer, `__rt.box(S).buf`, which is what the run-time
+`slice::from_ref(&S)` and a raw pointer constant to `S` name, so `ptr::eq(SL.as_ptr(), &S)` holds
+and a whole-element store through a `&mut [T]` reaches `S` and back. The offset names the first
+element at the element's stride. A `&str` into a static (what `str::from_utf8(&BYTES)` makes) is
+the text of those bytes, read from the static's initializer, and is refused when the static can be
+written (`static mut`, interior mutability); like the `&str` the same call builds at run time it is
+a JavaScript string with no place, so `ptr::eq(TEXT.as_ptr(), BYTES.as_ptr())` is false for both
+(true natively). Emitting the pointer names the binding and reads it only for an aggregate
+static's slot (`__rt.box(S)`, as a raw pointer constant to `S` already does), so the slice depends
+on initialisation order only as a reference to `S` does. A global reference in JIR carries
+no length, so the encoder declines a wide pointer to a static and the item takes the baseline
+lane; in a body that needs semantic cleanup lowering (the release profiles) that decline is a
+compile-time refusal, which is how a `const C: &[T] = &STATIC` used in such a body fails.
+
+**A closure as a `fn` pointer** in constant data (`const F: fn(u32) -> u32 = |x| x + 7`, the same
+inside `Option`, a table or a struct, and the vtable `core::task::Waker::noop()` builds). Const
+evaluation stores it as the closure's `FnOnce` instance (its body, or the `call_once` shim of a `Fn`
+or `FnMut` closure), which takes the environment first, and a `fn` pointer is called without one;
+printed as the bare function, a call handed its first argument to the environment slot (main read
+`0` for `F(1)`, and `Some(|x| x + 7)` did not match `Some`). The constant is the wrapper the
+run-time `ClosureFnPointer` coercion builds, `($a0, ..) => shim(undefined, $a0, ..)` with one
+parameter per input of the `fn` type, in both lanes: the baseline recognizes the instance
+(`constant.rs::reified_closure_type`); the JIR encoder describes the closure's type, whose
+`fn_pointer_shim` in the type table is that same item, and the link-time lowering prints the wrapper
+for a function global it finds there. The same instance reified through the trait method
+(`<F as Fn<(u32,)>>::call`, `FnOnce::call_once`) is an `extern "rust-call"` pointer that takes the
+environment explicitly, and stays the function itself. A closure at a type that is not a `fn`
+pointer is refused. A named function in constant data is the function itself, as before.
+
+**Not decoded, refused at compile time.** A `&dyn Trait` in constant data — plain, or as an
+`Option<&dyn Trait>` that holds `Some`, whether its data pointer names a promoted value or a
+`static` — is refused ("a `&dyn ..` in a constant is a trait object, whose vtable rustc_codegen_js
+does not decode out of constant data"); an `Option<&dyn Trait>` holding `None` compiles and reads
+back as `None`.
+So are a pointer one past the end of its allocation, which names no element, and a thin pointer
+into the middle of a `static`.
+
+`examples/core-tests/240`–`249` pin every place a niche constant can live, every niche carrier,
+the absent-variant and single-variant layouts (and the run-time discriminant of the latter, 248),
+wide pointers into statics, and the trait-object refusal; `290`–`292` pin niche constants over a
+zeroed heap, one-element slices of a static, and closures as `fn` pointer constants.
 
